// A Unicode text library: UTF-8 encoding and decoding with full validation,
// UTF-16 and UTF-32 conversion, code point classification, case mapping,
// grapheme cluster segmentation, a canonical normalisation sketch, display
// width calculation and byte order mark handling.
//
// Every numeric constant is written in plain decimal because the charge lexer
// only accepts that notation. Where a value is conventionally written in
// hexadecimal the hexadecimal spelling follows in a comment, so the tables
// below can still be checked against the Unicode data files by eye.
//
// The library is deliberately table driven: classification, decomposition and
// width are all expressed as explicit range entries pushed into vectors at
// start up, and the hot paths are bit manipulations over those tables.

// --- Scalar bounds -------------------------------------------------------
//
// The Unicode code space is 0 .. 10FFFF with the surrogate block carved out of
// the middle of the basic multilingual plane.

// 0x10FFFF
// 0x110000
// 0xFFFF
// 0x10000

// 0xFFFD
// 0x7F
// 0xFF

// 0xD800
// 0xDFFF
// 0xD800
       // 0xDBFF
       // 0xDC00
       // 0xDFFF
       // 0x400
       // 0x10000

// The two-word form of a supplementary code point subtracts this bias before
// splitting the remainder into ten high bits and ten low bits.
// 0x10000
// 0x3FF

// --- UTF-8 bit layout ----------------------------------------------------
//
//   0xxxxxxx                                one byte,    7 payload bits
//   110xxxxx 10xxxxxx                       two bytes,  11 payload bits
//   1110xxxx 10xxxxxx 10xxxxxx              three bytes,16 payload bits
//   11110xxx 10xxxxxx 10xxxxxx 10xxxxxx     four bytes, 21 payload bits

// 0x7F
// 0x7FF
// 0xFFFF
// 0x10FFFF

                   // 0x80
                   // 0xC0
                   // 0x3F

               // 0xC0
               // 0xE0
             // 0x1F

             // 0xE0
             // 0xF0
             // 0x0F

              // 0xF0
              // 0xF8
              // 0x07

              // 0x80
              // 0xFF
              // 0x0F
              // 0xFFFF
              // 0xFFFFFFFF
              // 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

// A whole machine word of bytes at a time: these constants let the fast ASCII
// scan test eight bytes with two operations.
// 0x8080808080808080
// 0x0101010101010101
// 0x7F7F7F7F7F7F7F7F

// --- Byte order marks ----------------------------------------------------

// 0xFEFF
// 0xFFFE, never a valid scalar

             // 0xEF
             // 0xBB
             // 0xBF

         // 0xFE
         // 0xFF
         // 0xFF
         // 0xFE

// --- Named characters ----------------------------------------------------
//
// The scanners compare raw characters constantly, so each one gets a name.
// The escaped apostrophe character literal is not spelled here; the library
// keeps it as a one character string instead.

// The single quote and a few other awkward spellings live as strings.

// --- Error and status codes ----------------------------------------------

// Every way a byte sequence can fail to be UTF-8. The decoder reports the
// most specific code it can, because "invalid UTF-8" alone is useless in a
// diagnostic.

// ran out of bytes mid sequence
// a 10xxxxxx byte where a lead was expected
// 0xC0, 0xC1, 0xF5 .. 0xFF
// a lead byte followed by a non continuation
// a scalar spelled with more bytes than needed
// D800 .. DFFF encoded directly, CESU-8 style
// beyond 10FFFF

// The decoder is a small deterministic automaton. Accept is the only state in
// which a code point may be handed to the caller.

// three byte form, lead was 0xE0
             // three byte form, lead was 0xED
             // four byte form, lead was 0xF0
             // four byte form, lead was 0xF4

// What a lead byte says about the sequence that follows it.

// Result of sniffing the first few bytes of a stream.

// General category, collapsed to the groups this library actually needs.

// Grapheme cluster break property, the subset needed for the extended rules.

// Word break property, again reduced to what the word iterator uses.

// East Asian width, which drives the terminal column count.

// Canonical combining class buckets. The real property is an integer from 0 to
// 240; only the values that appear in the small table below are named.

// Which normal form a transformation targets.

// Endianness of a 16 or 32 bit code unit stream.

// --- Core value types ----------------------------------------------------

// A decoded scalar together with how many bytes it consumed. A length of zero
// always means the error field is set.

        error == DecodeError_None;

        error != DecodeError_None;

        value >= 0 && value <= MAX_ASCII;

        value >= 0 && value <= BMP_MAX;

        value > BMP_MAX && value <= CODE_POINT_MAX;

// The four byte staging buffer an encoder writes into. Keeping the bytes in a
// struct rather than a vector avoids an allocation for the common case.

